1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,216
The saved Week 17 plan identifies a high-acuity multisystem medical
capstone and a trauma capstone with multiple mechanisms and diagnostic
mimics. It does not include the actual examination cases or answer
key.

2
00:00:15,216 --> 00:00:27,960
The following scenarios are newly written practice examples. They
train prioritization, alternatives, reassessment, PCR and handover;
they are not predictions of the school examination.

3
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:45,144
The patient has sudden breathlessness, weakness and new confusion. A
family member reports a change from baseline, and a medication list is
present. No single diagnosis is supplied; the crew must identify
urgent threats and gather relevant evidence.

4
00:00:45,144 --> 00:00:58,920
Begin with immediate safety and primary threats. Obtain focused
history and measurements using authorized equipment, verify unexpected
signals and request the clinical support required by the current
pathway.

5
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:14,664
Do not anchor on a single alarming symptom. Consider respiratory,
circulatory, metabolic, neurologic and medication-related
possibilities as categories, then use the patient’s actual findings
and current pathway to narrow them.

6
00:01:14,664 --> 00:01:28,848
If altered mental status is part of the case, use the school-taught
assessment sequence and check for reversible contributors when the
current pathway calls for it. State what you know, what you do not
know and what should be reassessed.

7
00:01:28,848 --> 00:01:42,624
The patient is found after a fall, with visible bruising, confusion
and reported new weakness. The witness did not see the full sequence;
the patient’s medication card and chronic-disease history are
available.

8
00:01:42,624 --> 00:01:57,480
Treat the injury as real while asking whether a medical event could
have caused or contributed to the fall. Protect immediate threats,
obtain the event timeline and avoid dismissing a serious injury
because another explanation also fits.

9
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:12,384
A diagnostic mimic is a reason to widen the assessment, not a reason
to stop assessing trauma. Preserve the mechanism, any unknown
interval, observed injuries, patient trend and relevant history as
separate facts.

10
00:02:12,384 --> 00:02:26,472
Use the school’s current trauma and medical approach. Do not choose an
advanced intervention or treatment from this practice vignette; stay
within PCP authorization and escalate when the patient or mechanism
warrants it.

11
00:02:26,472 --> 00:02:37,584
For either case, chart the source and time of each important cue. Keep
the witness account, patient account, objective findings and crew
interpretation separate.

12
00:02:37,584 --> 00:02:51,696
The written record and SBAR/MIST handover should tell a consistent
story. Include uncertainty, the trend and the immediate next concern
instead of presenting a false sense of diagnostic certainty.

13
00:02:51,696 --> 00:03:03,048
The saved school plan calls for a student-led seven-question debrief.
These original prompts organize that reflection; they are not
presented as the school’s verbatim seven questions.

14
00:03:03,048 --> 00:03:15,720
Keep the debrief psychologically safe and evidence-based. Describe
decisions and conditions rather than labeling the learner; end with
one narrow drill and a way to observe the next attempt.

15
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:39,744
Make the medical case concrete. The adult is confused and breathing
hard, with glucose two point seven millimoles per litre, room-air
saturation eighty-eight percent, breathing twenty-eight and pressure
eighty-eight over fifty-six. These are fictional observations for
reasoning, not a new clinical cutoff set. Check measurement quality
while addressing the immediate patient threats.

16
00:03:39,744 --> 00:04:06,336
Low glucose is a treatable finding, but it does not explain away
impaired oxygenation or poor circulation. Protect the airway and
provide the indicated respiratory support while arranging the
authorized glucose correction. If the person cannot safely follow
instructions and swallow, do not put an oral treatment into an
unprotected airway. Seek the support the patient needs rather than
waiting for a perfect diagnosis.

17
00:04:06,336 --> 00:04:31,128
For the next simulation cue, stipulate that an appropriately
authorized clinician has completed indicated glucose treatment and
respiratory support. Repeat glucose is five point one, saturation
ninety-five with support and pressure one hundred two over sixty-four.
The patient is more attentive, but breathing is still laboured. Report
both the change and what did not improve.

18
00:04:31,128 --> 00:04:57,816
Reassess the airway, work of breathing, circulation, neurologic
findings and the measurement context. Ask about recent illness, intake
and medication use without assuming an overdose from a medication
list. Ongoing acute illness means this is not a simple recovered low-
glucose release example. Continue indicated care and time-sensitive
conveyance, with clinical advice when needed.

19
00:04:57,816 --> 00:05:20,760
A useful situation statement is: this adult remains acutely breathless
after correction of confirmed low glucose. Then give the sudden
change, relevant medication history, initial findings, actual actions
and repeat findings. Say that saturation improved with respiratory
support; do not present it as an untreated room-air value.

20
00:05:20,760 --> 00:06:19,968
An original handover using situation, background, assessment and
recommendation. Situation: an adult remains acutely breathless after
treatment of confirmed low glucose. Background: family reports a
sudden change and provides the insulin list; the complete cause is not
established. Assessment: initial glucose two point seven millimoles
per litre, saturation eighty-eight percent on room air, respiratory
rate twenty-eight and pressure eighty-eight over fifty-six. Repeat
glucose five point one, saturation ninety-five percent with support
and pressure one hundred two over sixty-four, but breathing remains
laboured. Recommendation: urgent receiving-team assessment of the
unresolved respiratory and circulatory problem, with ongoing
monitoring and support. Include actual treatment details and times;
these fictional values are not a destination algorithm.

21
00:06:19,968 --> 00:06:43,584
In the trauma example, a witness last saw the adult at usual function
at thirteen forty-five, then found the patient near the stairs at
fourteen twelve. Confusion and left-sided weakness persist. Glucose is
six point two, and the medication history includes an anticoagulant.
Keep the two times, direct findings and reported history separate.

22
00:06:43,584 --> 00:07:08,304
There is a real injury concern and a possible preceding neurologic or
other medical event. A normal glucose value does not prove stroke; a
visible bruise does not explain every neurologic finding. Obtain the
relevant focused assessment, protect immediate threats and seek the
appropriate time-sensitive trauma and neurologic pathway advice
without delaying care for a complete story.

23
00:07:08,304 --> 00:07:33,144
Continue the actual trauma assessment: address airway or breathing
compromise and significant bleeding, preserve perfusion and use the
indicated movement and spinal-protection approach. Do not apply an
adult low-risk clearance rule as if an altered patient with weakness
met every requirement. Reassess neurologic findings rather than
documenting only the first examination.

24
00:07:33,144 --> 00:07:55,608
The anticoagulant history increases the importance of communicating
possible bleeding risk, but does not establish the site of bleeding or
authorize a reversal medicine. Do not turn the practice case into
permission for an advanced intervention. Report the uncertain
sequence, persistent deficit, treatment response and destination
concern to the appropriate clinical team.

25
00:07:55,608 --> 00:08:15,816
A useful mechanism statement preserves both the incomplete fall
account and the possibility of a preceding event. Distinguish observed
bruising from suspected head or spinal injury. Include the focal
weakness, confusion, glucose and any subsequent changes, and state the
actual treatments and response.

26
00:08:15,816 --> 00:09:11,160
An original handover using mechanism, injuries, signs and treatment.
Mechanism: found near stairs at fourteen twelve after an incompletely
witnessed fall; last observed at usual function at thirteen forty-
five. Injuries: visible bruising and possible head or neck injury,
with the complete injury pattern still under assessment. Signs:
confusion and persistent left-sided weakness; capillary glucose six
point two millimoles per litre, not a demonstrated low-glucose
explanation. Treatment: report the actual airway, bleeding, movement-
protection and monitoring actions and the response, not an assumed
treatment bundle. Highlight anticoagulant history, the uncertain
sequence and the need for time-sensitive destination advice. Do not
label the case proven ischemic stroke or merely a mechanical fall.

27
00:09:11,160 --> 00:09:36,912
Use this five-part practice plan for the school five-minute
documentation target, not as a mandatory operational time limit. First
establish the event and evidence sources. Add focused assessment and
relevant history, then the actual actions and times. Finish the
response, current concern and accepted handover before checking units,
missing times and unsupported conclusions.

28
00:09:36,912 --> 00:09:56,616
Never invent a treatment time, examination finding, consent discussion
or successful handover to fill the form. Mark a fact as unknown when
it is unknown, and distinguish later information from what drove the
earlier decision. Necessary patient care takes priority over finishing
a practice timer.

29
00:09:56,616 --> 00:10:18,792
In the medical case, ask whether the learner noticed persistent
respiratory effort after glucose improved. In the trauma case, ask
whether the learner separated last-known-well from discovery time and
kept both injury and medical causes in view. These are observable
decisions, not personality judgments or a self-awarded examination
pass.

30
00:10:18,792 --> 00:10:40,296
Change one cue for the next repetition: for example, the witness
cannot confirm the last normal time, or a repeat measurement is
unreliable. Explain how that changes the plan and handover. The goal
is transfer of reasoning under changed evidence, not memorization of
these two fictional patients.
